WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.900
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:10.900
Today we are going to see if an AI can become
a good software engineer.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.350
Spoiler alert, the answer is: yes, kind of.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:17.560
Let me explain.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:25.360
Just one year ago, scientists at OpenAI published
a technique by the name GPT-3, and it is an

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:32.290
AI that was unleashed to read the internet
with the sole task of finishing your sentences.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:34.699
So, what happened then?

00:00:34.699 --> 00:00:40.530
Well, now we know that of course, it learned
whatever it needed to learn to perform the

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.370
sentence completion properly.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:49.309
And to do this, it would need to learn English
by itself, and that’s exactly what it did!

00:00:49.309 --> 00:00:53.700
It also learned about a lot of topics to be
able to discuss them well.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:59.049
For instance, we gave it a try, and I was
somewhat surprised when I saw that it was

00:00:59.049 --> 00:01:04.670
able to continue a Two Minute Papers script,
even though it seems to have turned into a

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.710
history lesson.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:13.159
It also learned how to generate properly formatted
plots from a tiny prompt written in plain

00:01:13.159 --> 00:01:14.479
English.

00:01:14.479 --> 00:01:17.670
Not just one kind - many kinds!

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:24.520
And remember, this happened just about a year
ago, and this AI was pretty good at many things.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:30.950
But soon after, a newer work was published
by the name Image-GPT.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:32.500
What did this do?

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:38.820
Well, this was a GPT-variant that could not
finish your sentences, but your images.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.820
Yes, really.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:46.840
The problem statement is simple: we give it
an incomplete image, and we ask the AI to

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.789
fill in the missing pixels.

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:52.770
Have a look at this water droplet example.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:58.609
We humans, know that since we see the remnants
of some ripples over there too, there must

00:01:58.609 --> 00:02:02.369
be a splash, but does the AI know?

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:05.909
Oh yes, yes it does!

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:07.090
Amazing!

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.000
And this is the true image for reference.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.560
So, what did they come out with now?

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:20.500
Well, the previous GPT-3 was pretty good at
many things, and this new work, OpenAI Codex

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:26.430
is a GPT language model that was fine-tuned
to be excellent at one thing.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:31.800
And that is, writing computer programs, or,
finishing your code.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:32.820
Sounds good!

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.780
Let’s give it a try.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:40.720
First, please write a program that says hello
world five times.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.030
It can do that.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:49.320
And, we can also ask it to create a graphical
user interface for it.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.220
No coding skills required.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:57.790
That’s not bad by any means, but this is
OpenAI we are talking about, so I am sure

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:59.670
it can do even better.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:03.280
Let’s try something a tiny bit more challenging.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.690
For instance, writing a simple space game.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:12.980
First, we get an image of a spaceship that
we like, instruct the algorithm to resize

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:14.900
and crop it.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:19.770
And here comes one of my favorites: start
animating it.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:25.030
Look, it immediately wrote the appropriate
code where it will travel with a prescribed

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:30.840
speed, and yes, it should get flipped as soon
as it hits the wall.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.780
Looks good.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:33.950
Will it work?

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.710
Let’s see.

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:37.480
It does.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:41.170
And all this from a written English description.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:42.580
Outstanding.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:47.590
Of course, this is still not quite the physics
simulation that you all see and love around

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:50.570
here, but I’ll take it.

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:57.700
But this is still not a game, so please, add
a moving asteroid, check for collisions, and

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.700
infuse the game with a scoring system.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:01.940
There we go.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:05.510
So, how long did all this take?

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:12.629
And now, hold on to your papers, because this
game was written in approximately 9 minutes.

00:04:12.629 --> 00:04:15.310
No coding knowledge is required.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:16.780
Wow.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:18.460
What a time to be alive!

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:24.970
Now, in this 9-ish minutes, most of the time
was not spent by the AI thinking, but the

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:26.190
human typing.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:29.760
So, still, the human is the bottleneck.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.940
But, today, with all the amazing voice recognition
systems that we have, we don’t even need

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:38.340
to type these instructions.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:44.190
Just say what you want and it will be able
to do it!

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:48.800
So, what else can it do?

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:54.070
For instance, it can also deal with similar
requests to what software engineers are asked

00:04:54.070 --> 00:05:00.710
in interviews, and I have to say, the results
indicate that this AI would get hired to some

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:01.710
places.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:07.700
But that’s not all, it can also nail a first-grade
math test.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.500
An AI.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:10.820
Food for thought.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:17.220
Now, this OpenAI Codex work has been out there
for a few days now, and I decided to not cover

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:22.800
it immediately, but wait a little and see
where the users take it.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:28.611
This is, of course, not great for views, but
no matter, we are not maximizing views, we

00:05:28.611 --> 00:05:30.950
are maximizing meaning.

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:33.950
In return, now, there are some examples out
there in the wild.

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:36.290
Let’s look at three of them.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:43.110
One, it can be asked to explain a piece of
code, even if it is written in assembly.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:48.850
Two, it can create a pong game in 30 seconds.

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:55.430
Remember, this used to be a blockbuster Atari
game, and now, an AI can write it in half

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:57.210
a minute.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:04.169
And yes, again, most of the half minute is
taken by waiting for the human for instructions.

00:06:04.169 --> 00:06:05.730
Wow.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:11.900
It can also create a plugin for Blender, an
amazing free 3D modeler program.

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:16.970
These things used to take several hours of
work at the very least.

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:23.220
And with that, I feel that what I said for
GPT-3 rings even more true today.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:30.580
I am replacing GPT-3 with Codex, and quoting:
“The main point is that working with Codex

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:36.560
is a really peculiar process where we know
that a vast body of knowledge lies within,

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:40.850
but it only emerges if we can bring it out
with properly written prompts.

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:47.330
It almost feels like a new kind of programming
that is open to everyone, even people without

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:50.060
any programming or technical knowledge.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:55.360
If a computer is a bicycle for the mind, then
Codex is a fighter jet.”

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:58.780
And all this progress, in just one year.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:04.680
I cannot wait to see where you Fellow Scholars
will take it, and what OpenAI has in mind

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:07.580
for just one more paper down the line.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:13.180
And until then, software coding might soon
be a thing anyone can do.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:33.120
What a time 

00:07:33.120 --> 00:08:04.550
to be alive!

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:08.180
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

